us":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://democracy.croydon.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-10-19","committee":"Scrutiny Health & Social Care Sub-Committee","time":"6.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://democracy.croydon.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-10-20","committee":"Scrutiny Children & Young People Sub-Committee","time":"6.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://democracy.croydon.gov.uk"}],"recent":[{"date":"2026-07-29","committee":"Cabinet","time":"6.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://democracy.croydon.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-08-26","committee":"Pension Board","time":"10.00 am","status":"New","webcast":true,"url":"https://democracy.croydon.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-15","committee":"Pension Committee","time":"10.00 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://democracy.croydon.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-15","committee":"Scrutiny Streets & Environment Sub-Committee","time":"6.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://democracy.croydon.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-17","committee":"Audit & Governance Committee","time":"6.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://democracy.croydon.gov.uk"}]},"news":[],"news_drafts":[{"headline":"Croydon's debt servicing costs force reliance on Government support to balance budget","lead":"Croydon spent \u00a382m servicing its \u00a31.5bn General Fund debt in 2025/26, according to its annual governance statement. The council required \u00a3108.7m of capitalisation directions from Government \u2014 permission to treat day-to-day spending as capital spending and pay for it through borrowing \u2014 to balance its budget. The external auditor has rated the council's financial sustainability Red, the lowest possible rating, citing significant weakness in the council's arrangements for debt and cost reduction. An auditor's statutory recommendation, carried forward from the prior year unchanged, states the council must work with newly appointed Commissioners to develop measures enabling reduced operating costs and return to self-sufficiency, and that \"a solution to the debt issue is vital\" to assess the council's capability to manage its finances sustainably.","iso":"2026-09-17","paper_title":"Progress Update and Annual Head of Internal Audit Report","paper_url":"https://croydon.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s72885/72885.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Croydon's \u00a31.5bn debt continues to block return to financial self-sufficiency, auditor warns","lead":"Croydon's external auditor has renewed its formal statutory recommendation that the council must solve its \u00a31.5bn General Fund debt to achieve financial self-sufficiency, with annual debt servicing costs of \u00a382m. The council is operating under government directions for failure to meet its best value duty and continues to rely on exceptional financial support, including capitalisation directions (government permission to treat day-to-day spending as capital spending to be paid by borrowing or asset sales). The Financial Strategy forecasts this reliance will continue through 2030. The auditor rated the council's Financial Sustainability arrangements Red, the lowest rating, citing significant weakness in cost and debt reduction. A separate issue has emerged: the schools funding deficit reached \u00a357.6m by March 2026, growing by \u00a329.4m in a single year, and the council must show by March 2028 how to manage the residual shortfall when a temporary government override expires.","iso":"2026-09-17","paper_title":"Progress Update and Annual Head of Internal Audit Report","paper_url":"https://croydon.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s72885/72885.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Croydon given lowest audit rating over continued debt and cost control problems","lead":"Croydon's auditor has rated the council's financial sustainability as 'red', the lowest possible rating, reflecting what the auditor identifies as significant weakness in the council's ability to manage its debt and reduce costs. The auditor's statutory recommendation from the previous year\u2014that the council develop a solution to its debt and return to financial self-sufficiency\u2014remains unresolved. The council continues to rely on government support: capitalisation directions (government permission to treat day-to-day spending as capital spending so it can be funded by borrowing) enabled it to reduce its 2025\u201326 budget gap from \u00a3136m to \u00a3108.7m. The council also faces a growing school funding deficit, accumulating to \u00a357.6m, with a \u00a329.4m overspend in 2025\u201326 alone.","iso":"2026-09-17","paper_title":"Progress Update and Annual Head of Internal Audit Report","paper_url":"https://croydon.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s72885/72885.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true}],"findings":[{"iso":"2026-09-23","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"App2 CIL CS Examiner Report","paper_url":"https://croydon.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s73039/73039.pdf","headline":"Infrastructure funding gap revised up to \u00a354 million in March 2026, nearly tripling from April 2025 estimate","detail":"The Infrastructure Funding Gap Statement was updated from April 2025 figures of \u00a368 million in required infrastructure costs against \u00a347 million identified funding (\u00a321 million gap) to March 2026 figures of \u00a3133 million in required costs against \u00a378 million identified or committed funding (\u00a354 million gap). The update followed discussions at the CIL charging schedule examination hearing held in February 2026.","category":"capital","matters":"A material infrastructure funding gap of this scale represents substantial unfunded capital commitments that must be addressed through borrowing, reserve depletion, or service reductions. The fact the gap more than doubled signals either infrastructure needs were significantly underestimated or committed funding sources have not materialized as planned.","figures":"April 2025 IFGS: \u00a368 million cost, \u00a347 million identified funding, \u00a321 million gap. March 2026 IFGS: \u00a3133 million cost, \u00a378 million identified or committed funding, \u00a354 million gap.","quote":"Following discussions at the hearing, the IFGS has been updated (Document Ref. ED07) to a March 2026 baseline. It now identifies a total cost of over \u00a3133 million for all required infrastructure with identified or committed funding of \u00a378 million, leaving a funding gap of over \u00a354 million.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-23","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"Updated Draft - Cabinet and Council - CIL Charging Schedule","paper_url":"https://croydon.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s73037/73037.pdf","headline":"Independent examiner approved the revised charging schedule following formal examination and public hearing, confirming the rates are evidenced, viable, and compliant with statutory requirements.","detail":"The examiner's report issued 15 April 2026 concluded that, subject to two minor modifications, the charging schedule provided an appropriate basis for collection of the levy. The examiner confirmed the council had complied with CIL Regulations 2010, undertaken appropriate consultation, based rates on robust viability study evidence, and that the proposed rates would not threaten development viability or Local Plan delivery. The two modifications were: reduction of industrial/warehousing rates to \u00a335 per sqm, and introduction of nil rating for community/sport/leisure facilities by public or non-profit organisations.","category":"governance","matters":"Independent examination is the statutory gateway to adoption and removes legal risk of challenge. Examiner approval provides assurance that income projections rest on defensible, evidence-tested rates proportionate to development viability.","figures":"Public examination hearing held 26 February 2026; examiner report issued 15 April 2026; two modifications made to original proposal.","quote":"the Draft London Borough of Croydon Community Infrastructure Levy Charging Schedule Review provides an appropriate basis for the collection of the levy in the area.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-23","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"Updated Draft - Cabinet and Council - CIL Charging Schedule","paper_url":"https://croydon.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s73037/73037.pdf","headline":"Revised CIL charging schedule substantially increases projected income from new development, particularly residential schemes in Croydon Metropolitan Centre, independently confirmed to remain viable.","detail":"The updated schedule introduces CIL liability on residential development in Croydon Metropolitan Centre (previously nil-rated) and increases rates on other development types. CIL income is allocated annually through the council's budget-setting process and contributes to the capital programme. An independent examiner confirmed on 15 April 2026 that the proposed rates provide an appropriate basis for collection and would not threaten Local Plan delivery or development viability.","category":"income","matters":"CIL is a primary source of capital funding for the borough. Material increases to rates and coverage directly expand planned capital investment capacity, subject to developments actually commencing. The capital programme 2023-2029 is structured around expected CIL income.","figures":"Residential CMC (10+ units): from nil to \u00a3225 per sqm; residential CMC (9 or fewer units): from nil to \u00a3300 per sqm. A Ruskin Square-equivalent development would generate \u00a39-13 million under the new schedule, compared to \u00a33 million under the previous S106 mechanism.","quote":"the Council would receive approximately between \u00a39million and \u00a313million in CIL funding for the residential portion of the development","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-23","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"Review of Council Tax Support Scheme 2027-28","paper_url":"https://croydon.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s73022/73022.pdf","headline":"Reducing Council Tax Support could decrease collection rates from 98.0% to 97.5%, potentially offsetting budget savings.","detail":"The Finance Director warns that reducing CTS awards to low-income residents could trigger payment defaults or avoidance. A 0.5 percentage point decline in collection represents material risk: on a large tax base, this could eliminate a significant portion of the \u00a33.6m\u2013\u00a311.9m savings the council is pursuing.","category":"income","matters":"A financially stressed council pursuing savings through cuts to low-income support faces a collection risk that could wipe out the intended budget benefit and worsen liquidity.","figures":"Collection rate could decrease from current 98.0% to 97.5%","quote":"The Council Tax collection rate could be impacted if any changes were made to the CTS scheme, since any decreases to discounts would increase the amount that residents on lower incomes need to pay for Council Tax. As an illustration the Council Tax collection rate could decrease from the current 98.0% to 97.5%.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-23","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"Review of Council Tax Support Scheme 2027-28","paper_url":"https://croydon.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s73022/73022.pdf","headline":"Croydon explicitly describes itself as 'one of the most financially stressed councils in London', citing this as the reason for the scheme review.","detail":"The Executive Mayor's introduction repeatedly emphasizes financial pressures as the driver for the review, stating the council must determine whether support remains 'financially sustainable'. The report notes that elected Members are 'obliged to give conscientious consideration to all available options to reduce expenditure', indicating the council is under obligation to exhaust cost-reduction options.","category":"demand","matters":"A council in severe financial stress presents heightened credit risk and may face constraints on service delivery, debt servicing capacity, or future borrowing.","figures":"Scheme costs circa \u00a339m per year with approximately \u00a320m borne by the Council for working-age residents","quote":"As one of London's most financially stressed councils, it is right that Croydon reviews whether the level of support it provides remains fair to taxpayers and financially sustainable.","material":true}],"editorial":null,"freshness":{"last_paper_iso":"2026-09-17"},"name":"Croydon","daily_post":{"iso":"2026-09-21","since_iso":"2026-08-22","distress":true,"headline":"Financial sustainability is identified as one of the Council's most significant strategic risks","lead":"When a council's audit committee designates financial sustainability as a top strategic risk, it indicates structural financial stress that will constrain debt service capacity and operational flexibility","also":["64 other findings in this window","14 papers caught"],"watch":"Scrutiny & Overview Committee on 2026-09-22","n_findings":65,"n_documents":14,"top_paper_title":"Appendix A Audit &amp; Governance Committee Annual Report 24-25","top_paper_url":"https://croydon.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s72867/72867.pdf","draft":true},"findings_total":190,"news_drafts_total":3}}